{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794166232,"nonce":"GHYfcmszU1aRLNPqYiyzpkhERn8=","title":"Mobile example on iPhone 12 Pro Max: what works, and what is still a problem (frame rate on sustained scroll, CPU, memory)","message":"Status of the 0.6.1 mobile work, measured on the iPhone 12 Pro Max with the release build of mobile/example. \"Owner\" means the owner tested it on the phone; everything else was measured here.\n\n\n- Pinyin and other CJK text renders in the input on iOS (owner).\n- Emoji sequences with skin-tone modifiers lay out as one glyph on iOS and Android, including after a following space (owner).\n- IME works on both platforms (owner).\n- Scroll glide and deceleration on iOS (owner: \"smooth and steady\" after the GPU work).\n- Incremental rendering on iOS and Android: a frame with no input, scroll or state change blits the cached static layer and re-records only canvas content. Idle frames: 98% cached on the emulator, 2.4 to 3.1 ms GPU per cached frame on the iPhone against 13 ms for a full repaint (owner: \"better\"). Taps, typing, keyboard and scroll checked for stale frames on the emulator. Detail in 36c7812.\n- Card, field and button shadows are back in the example (they had been turned off with box-shadow: none as a workaround).\n- Idle CPU went from about 42% of a core to 13 to 20%. A Time Profiler run showed two causes, both fixed in the working tree:\n  - tick_css ran apply_stylesheet_state_styles over the whole tree on every frame, and was called from both blinc_needs_render and blinc_build_frame. That selector matching (ElementRegistry::has_class) was 75% of the main thread at idle. It now runs only when the event router's hover, press or focus fingerprint changes, or after a rebuild or prop update (mobile_paint::StateStyleGate, used by iOS and Android; the desktop runner already gates the same way).\n  - The accessibility snapshot walked and serialised the whole semantic tree every frame (36% of samples after the first fix). It is now reused until a paint request is raised, and at most 500 ms.\n\n\n1. Frame rate during sustained scrolling with shadows on (owner: \"not consistent at 60\", drops when scrolling up and down repeatedly). An in-process harness flicks from the top through the keyframe cards into the inbox list and back: three flicks down, three up, each lifted at speed so it glides and decelerates.\n   - Shadows on: fps per 2 s window min 56.7, median 59.5; 11 of 33 windows below 59.\n   - Shadows off: min 58.5, median 60.0; 4 of 34 windows below 59.\n   - GPU time per full-paint frame (blocking wait after submit, so it includes the clock ramp and overstates the absolute cost): shadows on, median 36 ms; shadows off, median 13.4 ms. A cached frame takes 2.4 ms. Every scroll frame is a full paint, so the shadow pass is what pushes scroll frames past the 16.7 ms budget.\n   - Fix candidates: feature switches or a compact variant for the shadow shader (the core shader got these and they were the large win), drawing a shadow only as the ring outside an opaque caster, and a retained scroll layer so scrolled content is not repainted every frame.\n2. Idle CPU is still 13 to 20% on a screen whose only motion is two canvas spinners. What is left: compositor frame work and Metal submission every frame (try_render_with_compositor, composite_frame), and tick_css still runs twice a frame. Staging buffers churn at about 16 MiB/s at idle (2.8 wgpu-internal staging buffers per frame, 128 KiB and 32 KiB), which also shows up as continuous zero-fill page faults.\n3. Memory: physical footprint 137 MiB (85 MiB resident), flat over time, no growth while idle or scrolling. Metal allocations are 76 MiB of it. Expected parts: three full-resolution drawables at 1284x2778 (about 14 MiB each) and the cached static layer (another 14 MiB). Not yet attributed: the rest of the Metal allocations (primitive and glyph data sized for 20000 primitives and 50000 glyphs, glyph atlases) and about 60 MiB of process memory (fonts, tree, caches). Needs a capture that starts with the app (attach only sees allocations made after it), or a per-resource log in GpuRenderer.\n4. Idle runs at about 59 fps, not 60, with nothing moving but the spinners. Not investigated.\n5. Android numbers come only from the emulator; the Pixel 10 Pro has been offline since the skin-tone fix.\n\n\nTemporary diagnostics, not committed: an env-gated autoscroll in blinc_build_frame that feeds blinc_handle_touch, an fps, encode and GPU-wait log in blinc_render_frame every 120 frames (full and cached frames separately), and a switch in the example that turns shadows off. Launch with xcrun devicectl device process launch --console --environment-variables. The launch fails with EINVAL when the command that started it has already returned (a detached process loses its terminal); run it in the foreground. Profiles: xctrace record --template 'Time Profiler', 'Activity Monitor' or 'Game Memory', attached by pid.\n\nRelated: 36c7812 (GPU and incremental rendering), 3c7afb9 (damage rect path), 9e11ba3 (class styles after incremental update).","files":null}]}